Goals
  * Setup local DNS filtering using Pi-hole.
  * Learn the security benefits of DNS in a live envrionment.
  * Reduce tracking and advertisements across devices on my home internet. 

Tools Used
  * Raspberry Pi 5
  * Raspberry Pi OS
  * Pi-hole
  * SSH from MacOS
  * Github blocklist
  * Google Nest Wifi

What I Did
  * Installed and imaged the Pi OS onto the Raspberry Pi.
  * Connected the device to my home internet via ethernet. 
  * Used SSH to remotely run commands and manage the system. 
  * Installed and configured Pi-hole.
  * Imoprted blocklist from github and added some custom domains as well.
  * Changed my home network settings to use my Pi-hole as the DNS server. 
  * Monitored live traffic through the Pi-hole dashboard. 

Why
  * Used Raspberry Pi becuase it is a low power, always on device. 
  * Managed the system over SSH to practice Linux adminstration,
  * Implemented blocklist to increase DNS filtering.
  * Reduce spam advertisements and analtyic tracking across home devices. 
  * Learn how DNS traffic works and look for anamolies. 

Problems
  * All DNS request were coming from a single IP address. 
      - All traffic in my Pi-hole dashboard was coming from a single IP, which I knew wasn't right. 
        After some troubleshooting, I identifed that my router was forwarding DNS request to my Pi, causing it to see all request from a single IP. 
        Becuase of the router I own, I cannot implement port forwarding, so i had to manually set the DNS on all of my devices. 
        It is important to see what request come from which device for security and troubleshooting issues. 

Learned
  * How DNS works and insight into network traffic flows. 
  * How DNS filtering can be a useful security tool.
  * How to manage Linux devices remotely using SSH.
  * The volume of DNS request that are generated daily. 
